   "text": "I read the structure as a venetoclax-type BCL-2 inhibitor in which the usual dimethylcyclohexene is replaced by a spiro[3.5]nonene.\n\n**Regions of the drawing**\n- **Left end:** a cyclobutane is spiro-fused to a cyclohexene. The ring double bond carries a 4-chlorophenyl group on one carbon and a CH2 link to the piperazine on the other.\n- **Centre:** the piperazine joins a benzamide ring at the para position to the carbonyl. The ortho position carries an O-linked 1H-pyrrolo[2,3-b]pyridin-5-yl group.\n- **Acyl sulfonamide:** the amide nitrogen is bonded to a sulfonyl group (C(=O)NS(=O)(=O)) that sits on a second benzene ring. That ring has a nitro group ortho to an NH-CH2 substituent.\n- **Right end:** the CH2 leads to the 2-position of a 1,4-dioxane ring. The hashed bond to that ring carbon is the only stereo marker in the drawing.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\nThe stereocentre is the dioxane C2. I took the hashed CH2 as pointing away from the viewer, so the implicit H points toward the viewer. The CIP priorities are ring O > ring CH2\u2013O > CH2\u2013N > H. Viewed from the front, O \u2192 ring CH2 \u2192 CH2N runs clockwise, and the H points toward the viewer, so the configuration is S. In the SMILES below, `NC[C@H]3COCCO3` encodes S.\n\nI could not tell from the image which end of the hash is the narrow one. I assumed the narrow end is at the stereocentre, as is usual.\n\nSMILES: O=C(NS(=O)(=O)c1ccc(NC[C@H]2COCCO2)c([N+](=O)[O-])c1)c1ccc(N2CCN(CC3=C(c4ccc(Cl)cc4)CC4(CCC3)CCC4)CC2)cc1Oc1cnc2[nH]ccc2c1\n\nCheck: this SMILES is not valid as written, because I garbled the spiro ring. The cyclohexene should be C3=C(Ar)CC4(CCC3)... with the ring closing on the ring atoms, which gives a six-membered ring (C3, C(Ar), CH2, spiro C, CH2, CH2) but the cyclobutane written as CCC4 is a six-atom path in the wrong place. The corrected spiro fragment is below.\n\nSMILES: O=C(NS(=O)(=O)c1ccc(NC[C@H]2COCCO2)c([N+](=O)[O-])c1)c1ccc(N2CCN(CC3=C(c4ccc(Cl)cc4)CC4(CCC4)CC3)CC2)cc1Oc1cnc2[nH]ccc2c1"
